  <notes-header>

  <chapter*|A constructive introduction to quantum field theory>

  <notes-abstract|Lectures at SISSA, Trieste, 20 October--6 November 2026,
  as part of the Probability and Stochastic Analysis Trimester Programme.>

  <with|font-series|bold|Lecturer.> Massimiliano Gubinelli (University of
  Oxford).

  This course is part of the <hlink|Probability and Stochastic Analysis
  Trimester Programme|https://sites.google.com/view/probsissa> at SISSA.

  <subsection*|Abstract>

  In these lectures I will review some mathematical problems associated to
  the definition and construction of quantum field theories. Mainly from
  the probabilistic viewpoint but also paying some attention to the quantum
  aspects. The goal is to give a wide perspective on the open problems and
  opportunities for research, in the era of large language models.

  <subsection*|Lecture materials>

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 1> (20 October 2026): Relativistic
    quantum fields and imaginary time. Wightman axioms and quantum fields
    as operator-valued distributions; the two-point function; positivity
    of energy and continuation to imaginary time; the Feynman--Kac
    representation, lattice spin systems and the Osterwalder--Schrader
    axioms.
    [<notes-store|slides (PDF)|sissa/2026/cqft_slides_L1.pdf>]
    [<notes-store|script (PDF)|sissa/2026/cqft_notes_L1.pdf>]
    Slides and script revised 21 October 2026.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 2> (22 October 2026): The Gaussian
    free field. Construction on the torus and Euclidean space; reflection
    positivity and the free relativistic field; Wick products; Gaussian
    integration by parts and the Dyson--Schwinger equation; parabolic and
    elliptic stochastic quantisation.
    [<notes-store|slides (PDF)|sissa/2026/cqft_slides_L2.pdf>]
    [<notes-store|script (PDF)|sissa/2026/cqft_notes_L2.pdf>]
  </itemize>

  <subsection*|Provisional lecture plans>

  The following plans are provisional and may be adjusted as the course
  progresses.

  <\itemize>
    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lecture 3: Stochastic observables, the
    Polchinski flow and forward--backward equations.> Decomposition of the
    field by resolution and stochastic observables; effective potentials
    and the Polchinski equation; forward--backward stochastic differential
    equations; the Wetterich equation and the variational structure of the
    flow. Further topics, time permitting: products of observables,
    perturbation theory and counterterms, and Dyson--Schwinger equations
    at every scale.

    <item><with|font-series|bold|Lectures 4--6: An FBSDE construction of
    <math|\<phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on the torus.>
  </itemize>

  <subsection*|Schedule>

  All dates are in 2026; times are local to SISSA, Trieste.

